  "title": "Spectral Lake 3D · Shader",
  "type": "web",
  "file": "index.html",
  "description": "A wide-vista 160x160 GLSL spectral lake with one shared curved screen for both the album card and the lyrics. The screen has a fixed chord length and its two ends curl toward the viewer, so the perspective projection itself produces the near-large-far-small look: content in the middle sits furthest away and reads smallest, the ends come closer and grow, and lines and covers arch naturally. Curvature, orbit along the wall and overall scale are independent controls, and the cover, title, progress line and lyrics all share the same surface equation. The square album object drifts gently like a leaf on water, nudged by low-frequency energy. Music-only mode hides the card and the lyrics unless a music session is actually playing: the media contentType reported by Wallpaper Engine filters out video and image sessions (paused video apps otherwise keep their title in the Windows media session and steal the card), and paused or stopped playback sinks the whole band away after a short grace period, then floats back when playback resumes. When nothing is playing the card is not shown with placeholder text at all: empty sessions and placeholder titles are treated as no media, and a very faint breathing beacon can take its place instead (optional), so an idle desktop is just the lake and a distant light. Two-line lyrics load local LRC first (explicit path, wallpaper lyrics folder, configured folders, then the last successful match cached locally) and fall back to online matching: several query spellings (raw title, cleaned title, title only) across every working lyric source, skipping empty and instrumental-only results, with a no-timestamp fallback. Failed lookups retry automatically, and non-music sessions are never searched. Credit lines at the start are shown by default and can be hidden. Synchronized to the real media timeline. No meteors or scrolling history.",
  "contentrating": "Everyone",
